  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/10b-catfishes-suckermouths-plecos-and-l-numbers/chaetostoma_pearsei_l_187_en/",
      "html": "<p>This Chaetostoma species is imported at regular intervals from the upper Orinoco through Puerto Ayacucho. Origin: Colombia, Venezuela.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "habitat",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/10b-catfishes-suckermouths-plecos-and-l-numbers/chaetostoma_pearsei_l_187_en/",
      "html": "<p>C. pearsei lives in fast-flowing mountain brooks and therefore needs oxygen-rich water in the aquarium.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "aquariumSize",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/10b-catfishes-suckermouths-plecos-and-l-numbers/chaetostoma_pearsei_l_187_en/",
      "html": "<p>This species does not grow above 10 cm total length.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/10b-catfishes-suckermouths-plecos-and-l-numbers/chaetostoma_pearsei_l_187_en/",
      "html": "<p>It is a typical aufwuchs eater, which means that in nature it grazes algae but eats mainly the crustaceans that live in the vegetation. In the aquarium it also eats flake and tablet food.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/10b-catfishes-suckermouths-plecos-and-l-numbers/chaetostoma_pearsei_l_187_en/",
      "html": "<p>C. pearsei is a friendly fish but can become territorial against individuals of the same species. If one wants to keep more animals in the tank, hiding places are obligatory.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/10b-catfishes-suckermouths-plecos-and-l-numbers/chaetostoma_pearsei_l_187_en/",
      "html": "<p>This catfish is very difficult to differentiate from L 147 and L 188. L 188 has often been described in the literature as C. nudirostre.</p>"
    }
  ],
